/-
# Finite bases for the identities of metabelian groups (Kourovka Notebook Problem 1.85)

Kourovka Notebook, Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 1.85 (A. L. Shmel'kin): is it true that the
identity relations of a metabelian group have a finite basis?
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.Data.Finset.Defs
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.FreeGroup.Basic

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.MetabelianLawsFinitelyBasedKourovka1Problem85Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 1.85 (A. L.
Shmel'kin). UnsolvedMath record UM-00700074 (`KOU-1.85`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

A metabelian group is one whose commutator subgroup is abelian, that is, any two commutators
commute. A finite basis of the identities of `G` is a finite set of identities of `G` from which all
identities of `G` follow: every group satisfying the finite set satisfies every identity of `G`.
Consequence is tested on groups in `Type`, which suffices because a variety is determined by its
relatively free group of countable rank.
-/

/-- The group `H` satisfies the identity `w`, a word in the variables `x₀, x₁, …`. -/
def SatisfiesLaw (H : Type*) [Group H] (w : FreeGroup ℕ) : Prop :=
  ∀ f : ℕ → H, FreeGroup.lift f w = 1

/-- `G` is metabelian: its commutator subgroup is abelian, that is, any two commutators commute. -/
def IsMetabelian (G : Type*) [Group G] : Prop :=
  ∀ a b c d : G, Commute (a * b * a⁻¹ * b⁻¹) (c * d * c⁻¹ * d⁻¹)

/-- Kourovka 1.85 (A. L. Shmel'kin): the identities of every metabelian group `G` have a finite
basis: finitely many identities of `G` imply all of them, in the sense that every group satisfying
the finitely many identities satisfies every identity of `G`. -/
def metabelianLawsFinitelyBased : Prop :=
  ∀ (G : Type) [Group G], IsMetabelian G → ∃ F : Finset (FreeGroup ℕ),
    (∀ w ∈ F, SatisfiesLaw G w) ∧ ∀ (H : Type) [Group H], (∀ w ∈ F, SatisfiesLaw H w) →
      ∀ w : FreeGroup ℕ, SatisfiesLaw G w → SatisfiesLaw H w

/--
Resolved true: the Kourovka Archive answers "Yes, it is", citing D. E. Cohen (1967), who proved that
every variety of metabelian groups is finitely based; the variety generated by a metabelian group
`G` is metabelian, so the identities of `G` have a finite basis. Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D.
Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue,
arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 1.85 (A. L. Shmel'kin); resolution:
D. E. Cohen, J. Algebra 5 (1967), 267-273, Kourovka Archive, Problem 1.85 ("Yes, it is").
-/
public theorem_wanted metabelianLawsFinitelyBased_holds : metabelianLawsFinitelyBased

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.MetabelianLawsFinitelyBasedKourovka1Problem85Wanted
